<Base
  title="Work · Andreas Jackson"
  description="Professional experience: EY assurance, three years of strategy consulting for the Gates Foundation, IKEA and T-Mobile, analytics at Edison International, and early-stage startups in London and Tokyo."
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="prose-wrap prose">
      <p class="eyebrow">Work</p>
      <h1>Where I've worked</h1>
      <p>
        Audit-grade rigor from EY, three years of strategy consulting delivered
        to household-name clients, hands-on analytics inside a Fortune 500
        utility, and early-stage product work at startups in London and Tokyo.
        The full detail is in my <a href="/Andreas_Jackson_Resume.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">resume</a>.
        These are the stories I'd tell first.
      </p>

      <h2>Ernst &amp; Young</h2>
      <p class="role-line">
        FSO Assurance Associate, Banking &amp; Capital Markets · San Francisco ·
        2026 to present. Previously FSO Assurance Intern, Wealth &amp; Asset
        Management, 2024.
      </p>
      <p>
        I work in EY's financial services assurance practice, analyzing
        financial statements, reconciliations, and internal controls across 3 to
        4 concurrent banking engagements. The job is finding the variances and
        material findings that matter and writing them up in risk summaries that
        client CFOs and leadership act on. It's where I learned that a number
        isn't done until you can show how it was built.
      </p>

      <h2>ABA Consulting</h2>
      <p class="role-line">
        Marketing Analyst to Senior Advisor · Berkeley · 2022 to 2025
      </p>
      <p>
        Three years and four roles at a student consulting organization running
        real engagements for real clients. I moved from analyst to project
        manager to senior advisor, delivering work for T-Mobile, IKEA, The
        Assistance Fund, and the Gates Foundation, and eventually owning the
        recruiting pipeline and training curriculum for the organization
        itself: 80+ candidates interviewed and workshops for 80+ members.
        That story has <a href="/projects/aba-consulting-ops">its own page</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Bill &amp; Melinda Gates Foundation</h2>
      <p class="role-line">
        Strategy Project Manager (contract, via ABA Consulting) · 2024
      </p>
      <p>
        The Foundation needed to decide which partner organizations could
        actually carry a major initiative. I built a quantitative
        partner-scoring framework from scratch, grounded in 30+ peer-reviewed
        sources and 10+ expert interviews, then led a cross-functional team of
        8 analysts across 5 simultaneous workstreams to evaluate 6 major French
        nonprofit partners. Six months of research came down to one deliverable:
        a ranked shortlist and an actionable investment thesis, presented
        directly to the Foundation's Manager of Strategy.
      </p>

      <h2>Southern California Edison</h2>
      <p class="role-line">Asset Data &amp; Strategy Implementation Intern · 2023</p>
      <p>
        Inside Edison International's asset strategy group, I designed an Excel
        operations database that automated the team's efficiency and headcount
        modeling, improving team productivity by 75%, and built Power BI
        dashboards that gave leadership a live view of asset KPIs. I also
        worked in SAP on internal reporting and asset data workflows. Small
        team, big company, and a lesson in how much leverage one good internal
        tool creates.
      </p>

      <h2>Startups, in London and Tokyo</h2>
      <p class="role-line">
        Emplio (London, 2022) · AI SILK (Tokyo, 2022 to 2023)
      </p>
      <p>
        Before the consulting work got serious I spent time inside two
        early-stage companies on opposite sides of the world. At
        <strong>Emplio</strong> in London I supported product and marketing for
        a UK startup still finding its shape. At <strong>AI SILK</strong>, a
        Tokyo company building electrically conductive fabric, I researched the
        digital health and metaverse markets, developed 7 product concepts for
        the material, and mapped a potential US expansion across 20+ prospective
        partnerships.
      </p>
      <p>
        Both were small enough that the work was real and unglamorous. They're
        also why ambiguity doesn't bother me much: at that stage nobody hands
        you a defined problem.
      </p>

      <h2>Also worth mentioning</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>IKEA (contract, via ABA):</strong> assessed
          financial-services market-entry feasibility, building customer
          segmentation models and competitive dashboards from a 1,500+
          respondent survey.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The Assistance Fund (contract, via ABA):</strong> led a team
          of 5 through a 20-week growth engagement with 100% on-time delivery,
          validated by 15+ expert interviews and a 3,000+ respondent survey.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>T-Mobile (contract, via ABA):</strong> customer and industry
          research on Gen Z recognition of their 5G product, built on 1,100+
          survey responses.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Education</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>UC Berkeley, Haas School of Business.</strong> B.S. Business
        Administration with data science coursework, GPA 3.7, Dean's List.
        Class of 2025.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

